# rocpiTech: technical characteristics and operation of safety controls (Annex I, 1.2), vehicle checks.
SEG = "BRK"
CAT = "rocpiTech"
Q = [
("A tyre pressure monitoring system (TPMS) on a truck or trailer warns you when:",
 "A tyre is losing pressure", ["The tread is fully worn", "A wheel nut is loose", "The axle is overloaded"],
 "TPMS sensors measure the pressure, and often the temperature, in each tyre. An early warning lets you stop before an under-inflated tyre overheats or bursts."),
("Electronic stability control (ESC) on a heavy vehicle helps to prevent:",
 "Rollovers and skids", ["Wheel spin while parked", "Overloading of the axles", "Brake fade on long hills"],
 "ESC brakes individual wheels and cuts engine power when it senses the vehicle starting to roll or slide."),
("On a truck with an advanced emergency braking system (AEBS), what does the system normally do first when it detects a collision risk ahead?",
 "Warn the driver", ["Switch off the engine", "Release the brakes", "Call the emergency number"],
 "AEBS first gives a warning so you can react. If you do not, it brakes partly and then fully on its own. You stay responsible for keeping a safe gap."),
("During a night drive your lane departure warning keeps sounding, though you never meant to change lanes. The most likely cause is:",
 "You are getting tired", ["The road is too wide", "The tachograph is in rest mode", "The trailer is empty"],
 "Drifting out of the lane without signalling is a classic sign of fatigue. Stop somewhere safe and rest rather than relying on the warning."),
("A truck's dual-circuit air brake system means that:",
 "One circuit still works if one fails", ["The trailer needs no air supply", "Both brake pedals must be pressed", "Brakes work only below 50 km/h"],
 "The service brake is split into separate circuits, so a leak in one leaves some braking on the other."),
("The brake pad wear warning lamp comes on in a truck with disc brakes. It means:",
 "The pads will soon need replacing", ["The discs are too cold to work", "The ABS has switched itself off", "The air tanks need draining"],
 "Pad wear sensors warn before the friction material is used up. Book the vehicle in promptly, as worn pads lengthen stopping distances."),
("The air dryer in the braking system removes:",
 "Water from the compressed air", ["Dust from the cabin air filter", "Oil from the diesel fuel", "Heat from the brake discs"],
 "The dryer takes water out of the compressed air before it reaches the tanks, which helps stop corrosion and freezing in the lines."),
("When you couple a trailer with EBS, which extra connection must be made?",
 "The ABS/EBS electrical cable", ["A second fuel line to the trailer", "A hydraulic steering hose", "The tachograph sensor lead"],
 "The trailer's ABS or EBS is powered and controlled through its own electrical connector (ISO 7638). Without it the trailer brakes have no ABS."),
("A warning lamp for the trailer ABS stays on after you move off. The trailer brakes:",
 "Work, but without ABS", ["Do not work at all", "Apply and lock at once", "Work only on the left side"],
 "A trailer ABS fault leaves normal braking but no anti-lock protection, so the wheels can lock. Have it repaired."),
("A load-sensing valve adjusts the braking force according to the:",
 "Weight on the axle", ["Outside temperature", "Engine speed", "Tyre tread depth"],
 "It gives an empty axle less braking pressure than a loaded one, so the wheels are less likely to lock when the vehicle is light."),
("On a long descent you use the hydraulic retarder hard for many minutes. What can happen?",
 "It overheats and loses effect", ["It charges the batteries", "It locks the steering", "It refills the air tanks"],
 "A hydraulic retarder turns braking energy into heat in its oil and the engine coolant. When they get too hot its force is cut back, so choose a low gear early and watch the temperature gauge."),
("An electromagnetic retarder slows the vehicle by:",
 "Eddy currents in rotating discs", ["Closing a valve in the exhaust", "Pumping oil through a turbine", "Compressing the air in the engine"],
 "Electromagnets induce eddy currents in discs on the drive line, creating drag and heat without touching the brake linings."),
("Front underrun protection on a truck is there to:",
 "Stop a car sliding underneath", ["Protect the radiator from stones", "Carry a spare wheel", "Reduce air resistance only"],
 "In a frontal collision the bar stops a smaller vehicle running under the truck, which greatly reduces injuries to its occupants."),
("Why are a truck's main mirrors set with the trailer running straight behind?",
 "So its edge is a reference", ["So you can see the cab roof", "So they show only the kerb", "So they fold in at high speed"],
 "With the combination straight, the trailer's side should just show at the inner edge, giving a fixed point to judge lane position and turns."),
("Your truck has cameras and screens instead of exterior mirrors. In your daily check you make sure that:",
 "Each screen shows a clear image", ["The screens are switched off", "The cameras point at the sky", "The old mirror arms are fitted"],
 "A camera-monitor system replaces the mirrors, so dirty lenses or a blank screen mean you cannot see. Clean the cameras and check every view."),
("After a long, hard climb, why let the engine idle briefly before switching off?",
 "To let the turbocharger cool", ["To top up the AdBlue tank", "To regenerate the particle filter", "To fill the air brake tanks"],
 "The turbo spins very fast and gets hot. A short idle lets oil carry the heat away, avoiding damage to its bearings."),
("In very cold weather, diesel without winter additives can:",
 "Wax and clog the fuel filter", ["Boil in the tank in the sun", "Give the engine more power", "Damage the AdBlue system"],
 "Paraffin in summer diesel forms wax crystals in the cold that clog the filter. Winter grade diesel resists this."),
("The battery master switch on a truck is used to:",
 "Isolate the electrics", ["Switch on the retarder", "Lock the gearbox in neutral", "Charge the batteries faster"],
 "It disconnects the batteries, for example when the truck is parked for long or in an emergency involving fire or fuel."),
("Your truck has an automated manual gearbox with a hill-start aid. The aid:",
 "Holds the brakes briefly on a slope", ["Selects reverse gear automatically", "Keeps the engine at full revs", "Replaces the parking brake fully"],
 "It keeps the service brakes on for a moment after you lift your foot, so the truck does not roll back before drive is taken up."),
("During the daily check you find an air leak you can hear at a brake chamber. You should:",
 "Not drive until it is repaired", ["Drive slowly to the next depot", "Drain the tanks and continue", "Turn up the governor setting"],
 "A leaking brake chamber can lead to loss of braking. Report it and keep the vehicle off the road until it is fixed."),
("A blind spot information system on a truck is most useful when you:",
 "Turn right at a junction in town", ["Drive on an empty motorway", "Climb a long hill", "Park in a truck stop at night"],
 "In right-hand traffic, cyclists and pedestrians beside the right of the cab are hard to see when you turn right. The system warns of them, but still check your mirrors."),
("Why must a heavy goods vehicle's speed limiter never be tampered with?",
 "It is a legal safety device", ["It controls the AdBlue dose", "It runs the tachograph clock", "It is part of the ABS"],
 "EU rules require speed limiters on heavy goods vehicles and buses. Tampering is an offence and removes a safety margin."),
("The marking \"M+S\" on a truck tyre's sidewall means it is suitable for:",
 "Mud and snow", ["Motorways and streets", "Medium speeds only", "Mounting on steer axles"],
 "M+S marks tyres designed for mud and snow. Tyres for severe snow also carry the three-peak mountain snowflake symbol."),
("A semi-trailer stands at a loading bay and a forklift will drive into it. What stops it creeping forward?",
 "Wheel chocks or a bay lock", ["The tractor's hazard lights", "Lowered air suspension", "The curtain straps"],
 "Each time a forklift enters, the trailer can be pushed forward. Chocks or a bay restraint hold it, together with the parking brake."),
]
